\tag{fdip}
\title{Realization and the information retained by observables}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{What is retained when a finite graph is replaced by a geometric shape?
A graph records points and the links between them. One way to give it a
shape is to assign an interval to each edge of its
[incidence presentation](fdip-0002) and join the intervals at their
endpoints. Three edges in a cycle give a triangle's boundary; the graph
alone does not choose its filling.}

\p{The [retained Gaussian law](fdip-000E) gives a sharper test of the
information preserved. Forget an edge weight while keeping its endpoint:
two weights can then give the same interval but different variances at
that endpoint. This is a small teaching variant of the
[weighted-cycle comparison](fdip-000I), governed by the existing
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042). An observable descends through
the replacement exactly when it is constant on the data that the
replacement identifies. Forgetting a weight can erase a retained
observable even when the geometric construction is well-defined.}

\p{The [enriched background comparison](fdip-000Y) preserves observables
only when the required data and the observables are transported together.
The examples below identify what is forgotten and keep scalar
factorization separate from categorical localization and gluing over
covers. The shape is built by hand, just as the preceding approximation
supplied its circle. Neither comparison supplies an emergent geometry or
gluing theorem.}

